---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHeader from '../../components/PageHeader.astro';
import Tldr from '../../components/Tldr.astro';
import TryItNow from '../../components/TryItNow.astro';
import CopyPrompt from '../../components/CopyPrompt.astro';
import KeyTakeaways from '../../components/KeyTakeaways.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import { href } from '../../lib/site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Effort Levels"
  description="The second dial after model choice: how hard Claude thinks, what each effort level costs, and the plan-high / execute-low workflow that saves real money."
  pageId="effort"
  readMinutes={6}
>
  <PageHeader
    icon="🎚️"
    title="Effort Levels"
    subtitle="Model choice answers 'which brain?' Effort answers 'how hard should it think?' Getting both dials right is where the real savings live."
    readMinutes={6}
  />

  <Tldr>
    <p>
      Higher effort = deeper reasoning = a lot more tokens — and we pay per token.
      <strong>Low and Medium handle the vast majority of work</strong> at the same accuracy as High.
      Cranking effort up on routine tasks usually buys you a slower, more expensive version of the
      <em>same answer</em>. Reserve High and above for genuinely hard problems — and for planning.
    </p>
  </Tldr>

  <!-- Two dials -->
  <div class="prose-content">
    <h2>The second dial</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={href('/models/')}>Choosing Your Model</a> covered the first dial: picking the right-sized
      brain for the job. Effort is the second dial — for the same model, it controls how much reasoning
      Claude does before answering. Low effort answers quickly and directly; higher levels think longer,
      explore more paths, and burn far more tokens doing it.
    </p>
    <p>
      You can pick the perfect model and still overpay by leaving effort maxed out on routine work.
      And the reverse is the superpower: the right effort setting makes a model
      <strong>punch well above its weight class for a fraction of the cost</strong>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Effort ladder -->
  <div class="my-6 space-y-3 rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5">
    <p class="m-0 mb-2 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">The five effort levels (relative token burn, illustrative)</p>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
      <span class="w-20 shrink-0 text-sm font-semibold text-text">Low</span>
      <div class="h-4 rounded-full bg-chat" style="width: 8%"></div>
      <span class="text-xs text-muted">quick, direct — great default for simple tasks</span>
    </div>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
      <span class="w-20 shrink-0 text-sm font-semibold text-text">Medium</span>
      <div class="h-4 rounded-full bg-gold" style="width: 20%"></div>
      <span class="text-xs text-muted">the everyday default ★</span>
    </div>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
      <span class="w-20 shrink-0 text-sm font-semibold text-text">High</span>
      <div class="h-4 rounded-full bg-code" style="width: 45%"></div>
      <span class="text-xs text-muted">genuinely hard problems, planning</span>
    </div>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
      <span class="w-20 shrink-0 text-sm font-semibold text-text">XHigh</span>
      <div class="h-4 rounded-full bg-code" style="width: 70%"></div>
      <span class="text-xs text-muted">the hardest architecture &amp; analysis work</span>
    </div>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
      <span class="w-20 shrink-0 text-sm font-semibold text-text">Max</span>
      <div class="h-4 rounded-full bg-cowork" style="width: 100%"></div>
      <span class="text-xs text-muted">rare — when a wrong answer is very expensive</span>
    </div>
    <p class="m-0 mt-2 text-xs text-dim">Not every model supports every level — Sonnet, for example, tops out below XHigh.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- Core insight + benchmark -->
  <div class="prose-content">
    <h2>Same answer, 7× the price</h2>
    <p>
      Here's the counterintuitive part: on most tasks — coding, refactoring, drafting, analysis —
      <strong>High effort doesn't produce a better answer than Low.</strong> It produces the same answer,
      slower, for several times the cost.
    </p>
    <p>
      This isn't just vibes. AI builder Morgan Linton benchmarked Fable 5 at <strong>Low</strong> effort
      against Sonnet 5 and another frontier model at <strong>High</strong> effort across 10 real
      engineering tasks. All three landed the same 80% accuracy. The bill:
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- benchmark stat card -->
  <div class="my-6 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <div class="rounded-[12px] border-2 border-gold/60 bg-gold/5 p-5 text-center">
      <p class="m-0 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">Fable 5 · Low effort</p>
      <p class="m-0 my-2 text-4xl font-extrabold text-gold">$2.27</p>
      <p class="m-0 text-sm text-text">80% accuracy</p>
    </div>
    <div class="rounded-[12px] border border-border bg-card p-5 text-center">
      <p class="m-0 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-muted">Sonnet 5 · High effort</p>
      <p class="m-0 my-2 text-4xl font-extrabold text-code">$15.81</p>
      <p class="m-0 text-sm text-text">80% accuracy — same score, ~7× the cost</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="prose-content">
    <p>
      Other engineers have confirmed the same pattern independently: running Fable 5 at High/XHigh burns
      through budget fast, while Low/Medium is more than enough for most day-to-day work. Going higher
      generally means <em>paying more to do the task slower, at the same accuracy</em> — not a better
      outcome, just a more expensive and sluggish one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Callout variant="gold" title="⚠️ Fable is NOT a cheap model">
    <p>
      The savings above come entirely from <strong>Low effort using fewer tokens</strong> — not from
      Fable being priced low. At $10/$50 per million tokens, Fable 5 is actually <strong>double</strong>
      Opus 4.8's rate ($5/$25) — the most expensive model we have. "Use Fable" is not the money-saving
      move; <strong>"use Fable at Low/Medium effort"</strong> is. Run carelessly at High/XHigh, Fable is
      our priciest possible way to get work done.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <!-- Plan high execute low -->
  <div class="prose-content">
    <h2>The power move: plan High, execute Low 🎚️</h2>
    <p>
      The highest-ROI pattern is splitting your work into two phases with different effort settings:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Planning phase → High (or XHigh).</strong> Architecture, high-level design, tricky
        decisions, writing the detailed plan. This is where deep reasoning actually pays off — and it's
        a small fraction of your total tokens.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Execution phase → Low/Medium.</strong> Implementation, refactoring, testing, iteration.
        This is where the bulk of tokens get spent — so lowering effort here is where the real savings
        come from, with no quality hit because the plan is already strong.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Pro tip:</strong> ask the planning model to output a clear, executable checklist. Then the
      low-effort execution pass just works down the list. Only escalate back to High for major revisions
      or when you hit a genuine blocker.
    </p>
  </div>

  <!-- Orchestrator + effort -->
  <div class="prose-content">
    <h2>Level up: let the orchestrator set both dials</h2>
    <p>
      If you're working in Claude Code, you can push this further. Remember the
      <a href={href('/models/')}>orchestrator pattern</a> — a big model conducting cheaper sub-agents?
      Sub-agents accept an <strong>effort setting as well as a model</strong>. So instead of switching
      dials by hand, ask your planning model to choose the right model <em>and</em> effort level for
      every step of the plan, and bake it into the sub-agents it dispatches: a Haiku agent at Low for
      exploring the codebase, a Sonnet agent at Medium for the implementation, an Opus agent at High for
      the one security-sensitive piece — all automatic.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Callout variant="code" title="Caveat: check effort compatibility first">
    <p>
      Not every model supports every effort level (Sonnet 5 doesn't go up to XHigh, for example). If the
      orchestrator assigns a level a model can't run, Claude Code <strong>silently falls back</strong> to
      the highest level that model supports — not necessarily what you intended. Explicitly tell the
      orchestrator to verify which effort levels each model supports <em>before</em> assigning them, so
      the plan is valid instead of getting quietly downgraded at execution time.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <TryItNow title="Try plan-high / execute-low with automatic routing">
    <p>
      Paste this into Claude Code at the start of a decent-sized task. It runs the planning at high
      effort, then delegates execution at the cheapest viable settings:
    </p>
    <CopyPrompt text="Plan this task at high effort: break it into an executable checklist of steps. For each step, choose the cheapest model AND effort level that can handle it reliably — but first verify which effort levels each model actually supports, and only assign valid combinations. Then execute the plan by dispatching sub-agents with your chosen model + effort per step. Only escalate a step back to a bigger model or higher effort if it fails or the result doesn't pass your review." />
  </TryItNow>

  <!-- Recommendations -->
  <div class="prose-content">
    <h2>The playbook</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Default to Medium</strong> (or Low for simple asks) for day-to-day work.</li>
      <li><strong>Plan High, execute Low</strong> on bigger tasks.</li>
      <li>In Claude Code, <strong>let the planning model assign model + effort per sub-agent</strong> — after checking compatibility.</li>
      <li><strong>Test before assuming you need High.</strong> Run the same task at Medium and compare — you'll usually find no difference but the price.</li>
      <li><strong>Fable is our priciest model per token.</strong> Reserve High/XHigh on it for work that genuinely needs it.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>

  <Callout variant="cowork" title="Where do I actually set effort?">
    <p>
      Effort is mainly a <strong>Claude Code</strong> dial — type <code>/effort</code> to see and change
      the current level (and sub-agents can set it per task, as above). In the Chat and Co-Work apps, the
      closest equivalent is toggling <strong>extended thinking</strong> for hard problems. Either way the
      principle is the same: more thinking costs more tokens — spend it where it matters.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <KeyTakeaways>
    <ul>
      <li>Effort is the second dial after model choice: Low/Medium delivers the same accuracy as High on most tasks — for a fraction of the cost.</li>
      <li>Plan at High effort, execute at Low/Medium. Planning is cheap and benefits from deep thinking; execution is the bulk of your tokens.</li>
      <li>Fable 5 is our most expensive model per token — it only saves money when you run it at Low/Medium effort.</li>
    </ul>
  </KeyTakeaways>
</BaseLayout>
